course: english
lesson: 17
title: Retrieval: finding the answer in the text
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A retrieval question asks for something the text says clearly. You do not guess and you do not use what you already know: you go back, find the words and use them. It is like a treasure hunt where the treasure is always in the text.
explain: Read this: «Tom walked to the shop at seven o'clock. He bought milk, bread and a newspaper.» Asked «What time did Tom go to the shop?», the answer is «seven o'clock». Asked «What did Tom buy?», you need all three things, not just one, and not why he went.
explain: The fast way: underline the key word in the question (time, buy, where), then scan, which means running your eyes quickly over the text looking only for that word or number. When you find it, read the whole sentence around it slowly. You do not need to reread the whole text for every question.
explain: Some answers need one small step. «The bus left at 3:15 and arrived 20 minutes later.» When did it arrive? Not 3:15: that is when it left. Add 20 minutes and you get 3:35. Tables, captions and headings are part of the text too: a table that says Monday 12°C and Tuesday 15°C tells you Tuesday was warmer.
fact: A retrieval question asks for information written clearly in the text.
fact: Retrieval answers come from the text, not from guessing or from what seems likely.
fact: Scanning means looking quickly through a text for a key word or number.
fact: Skimming means reading quickly to get the general idea of a text.
fact: If a question asks for several things («Name two things»), the answer needs every part.
fact: «Find and copy one word» means copy exactly one word from the text, spelt exactly as it is there.
fact: Some retrieval answers need a small step, like adding a time, but the information still comes from the text.
fact: Tables, captions, headings and bullet points are part of a text and can hold answers.
fact: Answers are often in the same order as the questions, but not always.
fact: Vague words like «and stuff» or «things» do not count as an answer; give the exact details.
fact: Using your own words is fine if the meaning stays exactly the same, but in tests the text's own words are safest.
fact: Slang: «heaps» (Australian) means many; «sound» (Liverpool) means fine or good; «skibidi» is a word some children shout that has no real meaning.
exercise: «Tom walked to the shop at seven o'clock.» What time did Tom go to the shop? | Seven o'clock | Eight o'clock in the morning | In the evening after his tea | Just before the shop closed
exercise: «He bought milk, bread and a newspaper.» Ellie writes «He went to buy milk.» What is wrong? | She missed the bread and the newspaper | Nothing, milk is the most important thing he bought | She should have written why he went to the shop | Milk is not in the text at all, so she guessed
exercise: «The fox lived in a hole under the old oak tree.» Max writes «In the forest.» Why is that wrong? | The text says a hole under the old oak tree | Foxes never live anywhere near forests | He should have drawn a picture of the hole | The text says the fox lived in a barn
exercise: Olivia in Sydney writes «heaps of birds»; the text says «many birds». Is her meaning right? | Yes, heaps means many, but the text's words are safest | No, heaps means a few, so the meaning is wrong | No, in Australia heaps means a big pile of leaves on the ground in autumn | Yes, and slang is always better in a test answer
exercise: «The crumbling house leaned against the wall.» Find and copy one word that shows the house was old. | crumbling | house | wall | against
exercise: Sam groans that the text is too long to read all of it. What is the smart way to find one answer? | Find the key word and scan for it | Read every word three times from the start | Guess the answer and move on quickly | Skip the question and leave it blank
exercise: «The bus left at 3:15 and arrived 20 minutes later.» When did the bus arrive? | 3:35 | 3:15 | 3:20 | 3:55
exercise: Holly in Liverpool says «Sound, I found it in the first paragraph.» What does sound mean? | Good, fine | Loud and noisy | Asleep | Sorry about that
exercise: A text says owls eat mice, voles and beetles. Leo writes «Mice and stuff.» What is wrong? | «Stuff» is vague: he needs a second exact thing | Nothing, «stuff» counts as the second thing | Owls never eat mice, so the answer is wrong | He should have copied out the whole paragraph about owls, word for word
exercise: Dad says, «Just look in the text, love, it's there.» What kind of question is this? | Retrieval | Prediction about what happens next | Opinion | Inference from clues
exercise: A table says Monday 12°C and Tuesday 15°C. Which day was warmest? | Tuesday | Monday | Both days were exactly the same temperature | You can't tell from a table
exercise: What does scanning mean? | Looking quickly for one key word or number | Reading every word slowly from the beginning | Copying out the whole text into your book | Reading the last line first
exercise: What does skimming mean? | Reading quickly to get the general idea | Looking for one word only | Reading aloud to a partner very slowly | Learning a text by heart
exercise: Where can retrieval answers be found? | In sentences, tables, captions and headings | Only in the very first paragraph of the text, never anywhere after it | Only in long sentences, never in tables | Only on the last page of the text
exercise: «Find and copy one word» means: | Copy exactly one word, spelt as in the text | Copy out the whole sentence that has the word in it | Write a word that means the same | Copy any two words
test: «miss the answer's literally first line, done, ez». Should you stop reading after the first line every time? | No, answers can be anywhere, so check the part the question points to | Yes, answers are always in the first line | Yes, the first paragraph has all the answers, because the writer puts them there first | No, the answer is always at the very end
test: Mia writes 3:15 for «When did the bus arrive?» What did she do wrong? | She copied when it left, not when it arrived | She added the 20 minutes the wrong way round | Nothing, she copied the number from the text | She should have written twenty past three
test: ★★★ Text: «Ana left home at 8:50. The walk to school took a quarter of an hour, but she stopped for 5 minutes to tie her lace.» When did Ana get to school? | 9:10 | 9:05 | 8:55 | 9:15
test: ★★★ Text: «Rain fell on Monday. Tuesday was dry but cold, 4°C. Wednesday was sunny, 9°C.» Kit says Monday was the coldest day. What's the best reply? | The text doesn't give Monday's temperature, so we can't tell | Yes, rain always means the coldest day of the week | No, Wednesday was the coldest because it was sunny | Yes, because Monday comes first in the text
test: «the fox lives in the woods, obvs, foxes always do». What's the problem with this answer? | It comes from guessing, not from the words in the text | Foxes never live in woods, they only live in towns, so the answer is untrue | It's too short for a two-mark question | It uses a capital letter in the wrong place
test: Max says, «Find and copy one word that shows the house was old: crumbling house leaned.» What went wrong? | He copied three words instead of one | He spelt «crumbling» wrong in his answer | He picked a word that isn't in the text | Nothing, more words get more marks
test: ★★★ Text: «Pip has two cats, Mo and Bea. Mo is black. Bea is grey with a white tail.» Q: «What colour is Pip's grey cat's tail?» | White | Grey | Black | The text doesn't say
test: In a cartoon, a detective solves a case without looking at the clues, from «a gut feeling». In a retrieval test, what works? | Finding the exact words in the text | Trusting a gut feeling over the words | Answering from what usually happens | Writing the longest answer you can
test: Dad: «Don't make me do it for you, where's it say that, then?» Why is this good help? | It makes the child find the evidence in the text | It's just a clever way for Dad to avoid helping with the homework at all | The child should know it without looking | It means the answer isn't in the text at all
test: Text: «Hedgehogs eat beetles, worms and slugs.» Q: «Name two things hedgehogs eat.» Which answer gets the mark? | Worms and slugs | Slugs and stuff like that | Things in the garden | Bugs and food
test: «skibidi this text is massive, i'm not reading all that». What does «skibidi» mean here? | Nothing, it's a word kids shout | It's slang that means the text is really brilliant | It means the text is too short | It's a type of question
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a retrieval question. | It requires information stated explicitly within the text | It invites the reader to conjecture what may happen subsequently | It solicits the reader's personal opinion of the text | It requires knowledge obtained from beyond the text
exam: It is stated that a vehicle departed at a quarter past three and arrived twenty minutes thereafter. Ascertain the time of arrival. | Twenty-five minutes to four | A quarter past three | Twenty past three | Five minutes to four
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the instruction «find and copy one word». | A single word must be transcribed exactly as it appears in the text | The entire sentence containing the word in question should be reproduced in full | A synonym of one's own choosing is to be supplied | Any two words from the relevant paragraph are acceptable
exam: Whereas a table records twelve degrees for Monday and fifteen degrees for Tuesday, determine which day was the warmer. | Tuesday | Monday | Neither, the temperatures being equal | Indeterminable from tabulated data
